```javascript
function characterFrequency(str) {
  const counts = {};
  for (const ch of str) {
    counts[ch] = (counts[ch] || 0) + 1;
  }
  return counts;
}

console.log(characterFrequency("hello"));
// { h: 1, e: 1, l: 2, o: 1 }
```
This treats every JavaScript string iteration unit as a character. For full Unicode grapheme handling, such as emojis composed of multiple code points, a grapheme-aware approach would be needed.

```java
import java.util.regex.*;
import java.util.*;

static List<String> findMatches(String input, String regex) {
    List<String> matches = new ArrayList<>();
    Matcher matcher = Pattern.compile(regex).matcher(input);

    while (matcher.find()) {
        matches.add(matcher.group());
    }
    return matches;
}
```
The method compiles the supplied regular expression, scans the input with a `Matcher`, and adds every matched substring to a list. If you only want to print the matches, replace the list with `System.out.println(matcher.group())` inside the loop.

